##DESCRIPTION
## Simplify expressions using trigonometric identities
##ENDDESCRIPTION

## Tagged by skm9b

## DBsubject(Trigonometry)
## DBchapter(Analytic trigonometry)
## DBsection(Using and proving basic identities)
## MLT(KentMoreLike5)
## Level(3)
## Static(1)
## TitleText1('Precalculus')
## AuthorText1('Stewart, Redlin, Watson')
## EditionText1('3')
## Section1('7.1')
## Problem1('37')
## KEYWORDS('trigonometry')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric')->variables->are(t => 'Real');

$f_cmp = Compute("sin(t)")->cmp(
    checker => sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $ansHash) = @_;
        return $correct**2 == $student**2 ? 1 : 0;
    }
);

BEGIN_PGML
Simplify and write the trigonometric expression in terms of sine and cosine:

[``` \frac{\sec t -\cos t}{\sec t}= (f(t))^2 ```]

[`f(t)=`] [_]{$f_cmp}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
[```
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\sec t - \cos t}{\sec t} & = (f(t))^2 \\
1 - \frac{\cos t}{\sec t} & = (f(t))^2 \\
1- \cos^2 t & =(f(t))^2 \\
\sin^2 t & = (f(t))^2 \\
\end{aligned}```]
So any function whose square is [`\sin^2 t`] is valid including [`\sin t`], [`-\sin t`] or [`|\sin t|`]. 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();        # This should be the last executable line in the problem.